---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../styles/pages/privacy.css";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy | Liberation and Inclusion Translation"
  description="The LIT Bible privacy policy. Your reading life is your own. The site collects personal information only when you choose to send it, and the companion iOS and Android apps keep everything you create on your device."
  canonical="https://litbible.net/privacy/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "WebPage",
      "name": "LIT Bible Privacy Policy",
      "url": "https://litbible.net/privacy/",
      "dateModified": "2026-10-13",
      "description": "The LIT Bible privacy policy. Your reading life is your own. The site collects personal information only when you choose to send it, and the companion iOS and Android apps keep everything you create on your device.",
      "isPartOf": {
        "@type": "WebSite",
        "name": "LIT Bible",
        "url": "https://litbible.net"
      },
      "publisher": {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "LIT Bible",
        "url": "https://litbible.net"
      }
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <article class="privacy-page" aria-labelledby="privacy-title">

    <header class="privacy-header">
      <div class="privacy-header__inner">
        <h1 id="privacy-title" class="privacy-title">Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="privacy-effective">
          Effective date: <time datetime="2026-10-13">October 13, 2026</time>
        </p>
        <p class="privacy-lede">
          The LIT Bible is built on a simple principle: your reading life is
          your own. This policy describes what data the LIT Bible website
          (litbible.net) collects, and how the companion iOS and Android apps
          handle data.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="privacy-body">

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-scope">
        <h2 id="privacy-scope">Scope</h2>
        <p>
          This policy applies to the LIT Bible website at litbible.net and the
          companion iOS and Android apps. The sections labeled “(app)” describe
          how the apps handle data. Where a detail is platform-specific (for
          example, iCloud sync on iOS or automatic Google Drive backup on
          Android), it is labeled <em>On iOS</em> or <em>On Android</em>;
          details that apply to both are labeled <em>On both platforms</em>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-collect">
        <h2 id="privacy-collect">What we collect</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Website:</strong> The site collects personal information only
          when you actively submit it:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Mailing list:</strong> If you subscribe, your email address
            is stored by Brevo, which manages the mailing list solely to send
            LIT Bible updates. You can unsubscribe anytime using links in
            emails.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Contact and app support forms:</strong> Messages sent
            through the contact form or the app support form (name, email,
            message, and on the app support form, which app you are using)
            are delivered directly to our inbox by Cloudflare, the site’s
            hosting provider. No separate form-processing service handles
            them. We use what you send only to respond to you; it is never
            sold or shared.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The contact and subscribe forms are protected from spam by
          Cloudflare Turnstile, a privacy-preserving alternative to CAPTCHAs
          that does not track you across sites. On the subscribe form,
          Turnstile runs in “invisible” mode: it verifies you silently in the
          background, with nothing to click or solve. See Cloudflare’s{" "}
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/en-gb/turnstile-privacy-policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Turnstile Privacy Policy Addendum</a>{" "}
          for how Turnstile itself handles data during that check.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Cookies and browser storage:</strong> The site sets one
          cookie of its own, a small flag (<code>lit_apps_launch_v1</code>)
          that remembers you have closed the announcement of the LIT Bible
          apps, so it doesn’t reappear. It contains no personal data and
          expires after 365 days. A few settings are kept in your browser’s
          local storage and never leave your device: your display settings
          (font, theme, text size, line spacing, what the page shows, and
          whether focus mode is on in Read View),
          whether you have turned keyboard shortcuts off, the place you last reached in
          each book in Read View, the chapter you last read (so the site
          can offer to take you back to it), and whether you have closed the
          Android app banner (which comes back after 90 days). A count of the pages you’ve viewed in the current tab is
          kept in session storage, which your browser clears when the tab
          closes, so the app announcement can wait until your second page.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The Study Desk preview:</strong> Readers who switch on the Study Desk,
          a preview of study tools for computers, can keep notes, highlights,
          bookmarks, labels, and sheets. These are stored in your browser’s
          IndexedDB on this device, and nothing about them is sent to the LIT
          Bible team or anywhere else. A copy you save from the notebook page is a
          file on your own computer. You can remove everything with “Delete
          everything” on the notebook page, or by clearing this site’s data in
          your browser.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Study Desk’s settings are kept in local storage under these
          names:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>lit-desk-preview</code>: whether you switched the preview on.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-panel</code>: whether the Notebook is open or closed.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-tab</code>: which of the Notebook’s tabs you chose last.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-min</code>: whether the Notebook is minimized.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-start</code>: how the panel opens on each page.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-show</code>: whether the Display menu’s “Show the Notebook” is off.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-notes</code>: whether your notes show.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-note-font</code>: which font your notes are shown in.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-bookmarks</code>: whether your bookmarks show.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-highlights</code>: whether your highlights show.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-read-highlights</code>: whether your highlights show in Read View.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-list</code>: whether My Notes shows each note in full.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-hide</code>: whether “Hide my marks” is on.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-home</code>: where you chose to keep the notebook.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-tour</code>: which steps of the notebook tour you have seen, and your answer when it was offered.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-page-tour</code>: the same for the Study Desk tour.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-sheet-tour</code>: the same for the Sheets tour.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-print-ask</code>: a moment’s note that you pressed Print, for the tab that prints.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-lexicon</code>: which lexicon you chose for the Greek tab.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-greek-notes</code>: whether Greek searches list your notes, once you turn that off.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-reading-off</code>: which display settings the Reading layout turned off, so choosing another layout can turn them back on.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-layout</code>: how you arranged the panes on the Study Desk page, your default arrangement, the passages its panes show, and which sheet each sheet pane shows.</li>
          <li><code>lit-sheet-paper</code>: whether you print sheets on US Letter or A4, once you choose.</li>
          <li><code>lit-sheet-answer-key</code>: whether the leader’s version of a sheet ends with an answer key, once you choose.</li>
          <li><code>lit-greek-commands</code>: that the window listing the Greek search commands has opened once, so it doesn’t open by itself again.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Session storage holds these until the tab closes:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>lit-desk-measure</code>: how wide you dragged the text column.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-width</code>: how wide you dragged the notebook.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-float-width</code>: how wide you dragged the notebook where it lies over the text.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-greek-split</code>: how you divided the Greek tab between the text and a word’s entry.</li>
          <li><code>lit-greek-commands-pos</code>: where you dragged the Greek search commands window.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-follow</code>: which bookmark is following you in this tab, once you start it following (from your list, its menu, or its dialog), so it moves with you while you read on in that book (held until you stop it or the tab closes, and let go after 12 hours away from that book).</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-release-feed</code>: a copy of the release notes, so a page doesn’t download them twice.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-note-draft</code>: the words of a note you are writing, so a reload doesn’t lose them.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The notebook also remembers, in its own storage:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>the date of the newest release note you have looked at, and how many changes it listed, to show what is new in your verses;</li>
          <li>the date you last saved a copy;</li>
          <li>
            whether it asked your browser to keep the notebook so it isn’t
            cleared when space runs short (some browsers show a prompt for
            that; the notebook asks again after a day if the answer was no,
            or when you press the button in Settings).
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Your open tabs tell one another about changes, and about a followed
          bookmark or a presented sheet, through your browser’s own tab-to-tab
          messages, which stay on your device. A sheet link someone sends you
          is held in that tab’s history entry while you look at it. The
          notebook is described on its{" "}
          <a href="/desk/help/">help page</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The site uses Cloudflare Web Analytics, which is privacy-preserving
          by design: “it does not use cookies, does not track individuals across
          sites or sessions, and does not build behavioral profiles.” Aggregated
          data like page views and general geographic region cannot identify
          individuals. Cloudflare, the hosting provider, may log basic request
          data at the network level for security and performance, subject to{" "}
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cloudflare’s privacy policy</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>App (on both platforms):</strong> The app does not collect
          your name, email address, device identifiers, or identifying
          information.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-reading-data">
        <h2 id="privacy-reading-data">Your reading data (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> Everything you create in the app
          (highlights, notes, and your reading position) is stored privately on
          your device. The LIT Bible team cannot access this data.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>On iOS:</strong> If you have iCloud enabled for LIT Bible,
          this data is also synced to your personal iCloud account via Apple’s
          CloudKit. Sync happens automatically in the background, the same way
          Apple Notes and Reminders work. Your data is stored in your own
          private iCloud database; the LIT Bible team has no access to it there
          either. You can turn off iCloud sync for LIT Bible at any time in iOS
          Settings → [Your Name] → iCloud → Apps Using iCloud. App preferences
          like text size and display settings are stored in your device’s
          standard preferences storage and are not synced to iCloud.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>On Android:</strong> If your device has Backup enabled, a copy
          of your highlights and notes is included in your device’s automatic
          backup to your own Google Drive (via Android Auto Backup), so they can
          restore on a new device. This backup lives in your personal Google
          account; the LIT Bible team has no access to it there either. You can
          turn it off at any time in your device’s settings under Settings →
          Google → Backup. Your reading position and your app preferences (like
          text size and display settings) are stored on your device but are not
          included in this backup; only your highlights and notes are.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-content-updates">
        <h2 id="privacy-content-updates">Content updates (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> The app periodically checks
          litbible.net servers for Bible translation text updates. During this
          check, your device downloads changed content with no information about
          you, your device, or reading activity transmitted to the servers. The
          process compares file versions and downloads updated text only.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-widget">
        <h2 id="privacy-widget">The home screen widget (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> The LIT Bible widget displays
          lectionary readings and liturgical calendar information. All widget
          data is computed on your device from information bundled with the app.
          The widget makes no network requests.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-external-links">
        <h2 id="privacy-external-links">External links</h2>
        <p>
          The website and app include links to external services. Following
          these links subjects you to those third parties’ privacy policies.
          The LIT Bible team does not track whether you visit these pages or
          take actions on them.
        </p>
        <p>External services linked include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Give Lively</strong>, for contributions to the LIT Bible and
            the Liberating Scripture Collective
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Apple Podcasts</strong>, for the Found in Translation
            podcast
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Spotify</strong>, for the Found in Translation podcast
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>YouTube</strong>, for the Found in Translation podcast
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>CreativeCommons.org</strong>, to display the full license
            text
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>liberatingscripture.org</strong>, the Liberating Scripture
            Collective’s website
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-sharing">
        <h2 id="privacy-sharing">Sharing (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> When you select text and choose
          Share, your device’s standard share sheet opens. The selected passage
          text is passed to whatever app you choose to share it with. The LIT
          Bible team does not log what you share, where you share it, or
          frequency.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-third-party">
        <h2 id="privacy-third-party">Third-party software (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On iOS,</strong> the app uses one third-party software
          library: <strong>GRDB</strong>, an open-source database library that
          stores scripture content locally on your device. It makes no network
          connections of its own. iCloud sync uses Apple’s CloudKit framework,
          which is part of the iOS operating system and subject to Apple’s
          privacy policy.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>On Android,</strong> the app uses two third-party software
          libraries: <strong>Room</strong> (an open-source database library from
          Google that stores scripture content locally on your device using the
          device’s built-in SQLite), and <strong>OkHttp</strong> (an open-source
          HTTP library used only for the content-update downloads described
          above, fetching translation text from litbible.net). Automatic backup
          of your highlights and notes uses Android Auto Backup, which is part
          of the Android operating system and subject to Google’s privacy
          policy.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-children">
        <h2 id="privacy-children">Children</h2>
        <p>
          The LIT Bible website and app do not knowingly collect personal
          information from children. The site and app contain no advertising
          and no social features. Any personal data the site receives, such as
          a newsletter subscription or a message sent through the contact or
          app support forms, requires you to actively submit it and is never
          required to access content.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-changes">
        <h2 id="privacy-changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
          If the policy updates in ways materially affecting data handling, the
          LIT Bible team will note it in the app’s Translation Updates section
          and on the website, updating the effective date above.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-contact">
        <h2 id="privacy-contact">Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions about this policy can be directed to the LIT Bible team at
          <a href="/contact">litbible.net/contact</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
